{{liushu|p}}. {{zh-l|*虍}} represents the tiger's head. The torso has disappeared and the legs and the tail have transformed into {{zh-l|*人}} in the small seal script and later {{zh-l|*儿}} in the clerical script.

===Etymology 1===
{{zh-forms|alt=乕,𢁺,𠂰,𧆞,𮓙,𮓜,𠪳,𢋪,𪋕,𧇂,虝,𪊖,俿}}
[[Image:Panthera tigris tigris.jpg|thumb|A tiger.]]

From {{inh|zh|sit-pro|*k-la||tiger}}, from {{der|zh|aav-pro|*kla(ː)ʔ||tiger}}. Cognate with {{och-l|菟|id=2}} in {{och-l|於菟|id=2,2|tiger}}. Compare {{cognate|vi|khái}} ("tiger"). 

{{zh-ref|Hill (2019)}} compares it to {{cog|bo|སྟག||tiger}}; however, Zheng Zining deems that comparison "probably spurious".<ref>Zheng Zining (undated) “''The Historical Phonology of Tibetan, Burmese and Chinese''. By Nathan W. HILL. New York: Cambridge University Press, 2019. Pp. xiv+373.” [https://jas.hkbu.edu.hk/content/ito/en/_jcr_content.ssocheck.json?pathPdf=/content/dam/jas-assets/publications/bulletin-of-the-jao-tsung-i-academy-of-sinology/issue-7-july-2020/10.pdf Book Review], p. 332</ref>

